Z||000003||Master_Sushi||11-23-2001||01:27 AM||||I was just searching throug the archives for stuff for engine flushing...<P>I was wondering, I read a few things that said you can gum the ¿oil pump screen? up with crud by flushing the engine. Just looking into the oil fill area on my duke I can see tons of crap in it. I changed the oil less then 1k ago also. Hmmmm...<P>Anyway, what happens if it clogs up? Do I have to take apart the engine or something. I really dont want to have to do that now! lol||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||11-23-2001||06:21 AM||||HUNH?! I would never use anything but an automotive oil-flush product, and I don't even use those.<P>Just drain your oil hot (well, warm, as in idle for a while, not HOT as in after flogging it HARD off the rev-limiter for half an hour  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Yeah, just drain hot, and change Mibil 1 oil and filter every 3k, and you'll be happy!<P>(note: I have now switched to K&N oil filters, as I can no longer get Mobil 1 filters here)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||11-23-2001||09:32 AM||||{{Trying to keep this thread at top becuase oil somehow really interests me}}<P>Which is better K&N oil filter or mobile one? The mobil 1 filter are 10$ at autozone, anyone know what the K&N are?||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||11-23-2001||10:11 AM||||Master_Sushi, If you did an archive search, you may have ran across my experience with flushing and plugging the oil pump screen. This happened to me because I had cleaned the top of the head while changing the valve cover gasket. (LOTS of crud up there from a couple years of blowby) On an eng with normal wear, I don't think flushing would cause any great problem, depending how you do it, but also don't think it would be necessary.  The flush I used after plugging the pump screen cautioned not to drive the car, just to run it a few minutes (10 I think) at idle, drain the oil, and change the filter. It is supposed to disolve the sludge and put it into a suspension in the oil pan so it can be drained out. I really don't think flushing would be necessary if your engine is in fair to good shape. I knew I had to rebuild, so I was just interested in making it last till I could get the rebuilt eng in. I drained the oil, poured a mixture of kerosene and diesel into the eng, let it sit in the pan do help dissolve the stuff off the screen, rocked the rear of the car around a little, then drained it out. Poured some more in with the drain plug out to help flush some of the crap out, then ran some flush according to directions, for ten minutes. I DON'T recommend this procedure for a good engine. I agree with Artherd about using anything but approved stuff for a flush.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula88||11-23-2001||12:33 PM||||I advise against flushing an engine with high miles, especially if you don't know the car's history.  If there's a problem you hope the flush with fix, fine...but the problem you can run into is the car may have a lot of buildup and gunk in the engine.  It may run fine like that, but when you flush it, all that gunk gets loose and not all of it comes out, so you've got this gunk circulating in your engine clogging oil passeges up.<P>If you intend to motor flush a high miles engine - I recommend also pulling the oil pan and cleaning it out  - also wash out the engine with a flush of fresh motor oil before putting the pan back on.  I'd also, do another oil change in about 500 miles to clean out any other debris that may still be floating around.<P>Some oils have a parafin base (i.e. Castrol) and the buildup can be quite extensive over time, but harmless to the engine (up to a point).  If all that gunk is knocked loose, and you don't get it out, you're asking for big engine trouble.<P>Just a warning.  Your mileage may vary.  I think you'll do much better by just doing more frequent oil and filter changes for a while than flushing the motor.  <P>The only other treatment I'd recommend is every once in a while, when you fill up your crankcase, substitue a quart of Marvel Mystery Oil for 1 quart of oil.  It's similar to a light machine oil and available at most auto parts stores.  It's quite good for top end lubrication and can help quiet sticking valves.  But, don't use more than 1 quart in your car. ||||reg||1
